From: Stained Glass OS <ke7oxh@gmail.com>
Subject: [PATCH] ntdll, wow64, kernelbase, kernel32: working set limits, pooled usage, critical processes

- GetProcessWorkingSetSizeEx was a FIXME that gave 32 MB and
  SetProcessWorkingSetSizeEx did nothing: they go through
  NtQueryInformationProcess/NtSetInformationProcess ProcessQuotaLimits.
  A process's working set limits and hard-limit flags
  (QUOTA_LIMITS_HARDWS_*) are kept and read back, Windows' defaults
  (204800 and 1413120 bytes, no hard limits) until set; a minimum over the
  maximum is STATUS_BAD_WORKING_SET_LIMIT (ERROR_INVALID_PARAMETER),
  enabling and disabling the same limit STATUS_INVALID_PARAMETER, both
  (SIZE_T)-1 trims (the host's to do) and keeps them. The limits are not
  enforced on the host. Another process's are its defaults.
- ProcessQuotaLimits was a FIXME answering only QUOTA_LIMITS; it answers
  QUOTA_LIMITS_EX too, with the flags, and gives the size needed when the
  size is wrong. Setting it was "stub" STATUS_NOT_IMPLEMENTED.
- ProcessPooledUsageAndLimits (unimplemented): the pool and page file use
  from the process's counters, no limits.
- ProcessBreakOnTermination (unimplemented): kept and read back; setting it
  takes SeDebugPrivilege (STATUS_PRIVILEGE_NOT_HELD otherwise).
- wow64: the 32-bit QUOTA_LIMITS_EX and POOLED_USAGE_AND_LIMITS, and
  setting ProcessQuotaLimits and ProcessBreakOnTermination.

Gate: test/procquota-gate.sh (64- and 32-bit, test/procquota-probe.c).
Mutants SG_MUTANT_QUOTA_NOT_KEPT, SG_MUTANT_NO_PRIV_CHECK
(ntdll/unix/process.c). Wine's ntdll info test: as before (one failure of
the harness's path); kernel32 process test: no failures (one before).
---
--- a/dlls/kernel32/process.c
+++ b/dlls/kernel32/process.c
@@ -368,7 +368,9 @@ BOOL WINAPI SetProcessWorkingSetSize(HANDLE process, SIZE_T minset, SIZE_T maxse
  */
 BOOL WINAPI GetProcessWorkingSetSize(HANDLE process, SIZE_T *minset, SIZE_T *maxset)
 {
-    return GetProcessWorkingSetSizeEx(process, minset, maxset, NULL);
+    DWORD flags;
+
+    return GetProcessWorkingSetSizeEx(process, minset, maxset, &flags);
 }
 
 
